{:edb/db-before-t 2043 :edb/db-after-t 2044 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535356 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:42:49.402Z" 13194139535356 true] [17592186149870 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149874 13194139535356 true] [17592186149875 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149877 13194139535356 true] [17592186149878 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149881 13194139535356 true] [17592186149883 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149885 13194139535356 true] [17592186151837 1100 "Consider the differential equation below, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2$, where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n$x^2y'' - 7xy' + 12y = -3x^3$\nDetermine whether $y_p(x)=x$ or $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation, and then write the general solution." 13194139535356 false] [17592186151837 1100 "Consider the differential equation below, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n\n$$\nx^2y'' - 7xy' + 12y = -3x^3\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $y_p(x)=x$ or $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation, and then write the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186151837 1145 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n- $y_p(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x$, we have\n$y^{′}(x)|= (x)^{′} = 1,; y^{″}(x)|= (1)^{′} = 0$.\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n$x^2(0) - 7x(1) + 12(x) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{red}\\times}$\nSo, $y_p(x)=x$ isn't a particular solution of the differential equation.\n- For $y_p(x)=x^3$, we have\n$y^{′}(x)|= (x^{3})^{′} = 3x^{2},; y^{″}(x)|= (3x^{2})^{′} = 6x$.\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n$x^2(6x) - 7x(3x^2) + 12(x^3) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\nSo, $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= Ax^{6} + Bx^{2} + x^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139535356 false] [17592186151837 1145 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(0) - 7x(1) + 12(x) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x$ isn't a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x^3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(6x) - 7x(3x^2) + 12(x^3) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{6}+B{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139535356 true] [17592186151909 1100 "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{5x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 10y' + 25y = 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 false] [17592186151909 1100 "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{5x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 10y' + 25y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186151909 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let\n\n$$\ny_{2}=u(x) e^{5x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2}^{′} &= u^{′}e^{5x} + 5ue^{5x} = e^{5x}(u^{′} + 5u) \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= e^{5x}(u^{″} + 10u^{′} + 25u)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{5x}(u^{″} + 10u^{′} + 25u) - 10e^{5x}(u^{′} + 5u) + 25e^{5x}u &= 0 \\\\\ne^{5x}(u^{″} + 10u^{′} + 25u - 10u^{′} - 50u + 25u) &= 0 \\\\\ne^{5x}(u^{″}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ne^{5x}\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = ue^{5x}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right)\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}\n$$\n\nFinally, because\n\n$$\nW(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}\n$$" 13194139535356 false] [17592186151909 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2}=u(x) e^{5x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}={e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u)-10{e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u)+25{e}^{5x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u-10{u}^{′}-50u+25u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{5x}\\neq 0$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = ue^{5x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186151911 1100 "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$." 13194139535356 false] [17592186151911 1100 "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}.$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186151911 1145 "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\ne^{x}  - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n4e^{2x}  - 3(2e^{2x})  + 2(e^{2x} ) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{x})}^{′}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0,$ the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III." 13194139535356 true] [17592186151911 1145 "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n$y_{1}^{′}(x)|= e^{x}; y_{1}^{″}(x)|= e^{x}$,\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n$e^{x} - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark$\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n$y_{2}^{′}(x)|= 2e^{2x}; y_{2}^{″}(x)|= 4e^{2x}$,\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n$4e^{2x} - 3(2e^{2x}) + 2(e^{2x}) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark$\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n$W(y_{1}, y_{2})|=\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}; = y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2}; = e^{x}(e^{2x})^{′} - (e^{x})^{′}e^{2x}; = 2e^{3x} - e^{3x}; = e^{3x}$.\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0$, the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III." 13194139535356 false] [17592186151912 1100 "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 false] [17592186151912 1100 "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186151912 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2}(x) &= u(x)y_{1}(x) \\\\\n&= u(x) \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n&= (u(x))/(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating\n\n$$\ny_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= \\frac{u}{x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= \\frac{u^{′}x - u}{x^{2}} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= \\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} + 3xy^{′} + y &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}(\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u}{x^{3}}) + 3x(\\frac{u^{′}x - u}{x^{2}}) + \\frac{u}{x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u + 3u^{′}x - 3u + u}{x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} + u^{′}x}{x} &= 0 \\\\\nu^{″}x + u^{′} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'$. Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}\n$$\n\nSince $w = u'$, we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}\n$$\n\nFinally, because\n\n$$\nW(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}\n$$" 13194139535356 false] [17592186151912 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{u(x)}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =\\frac{u}{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & =\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}})+3x(\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u+3{u}^{′}x-3u+u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x}{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x+{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186152216 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345436 13194139535356 true] [17592186152216 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345443 13194139535356 true] [17592186152216 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345568 13194139535356 true] [17592186152216 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345575 13194139535356 true] [17592186152216 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345596 13194139535356 true] [17592186152217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345422 13194139535356 true] [17592186152217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345429 13194139535356 true] [17592186152217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345554 13194139535356 true] [17592186152217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345582 13194139535356 true] [17592186152217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345603 13194139535356 true] [17592186152218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345450 13194139535356 true] [17592186152218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345457 13194139535356 true] [17592186152218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345464 13194139535356 true] [17592186152218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345561 13194139535356 true] [17592186152218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345589 13194139535356 true] [17592186152219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345471 13194139535356 true] [17592186152219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345491 13194139535356 true] [17592186152219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345511 13194139535356 true] [17592186152219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345531 13194139535356 true] [17592186152495 1017 #edb/ref 17592186149865 13194139535356 false] [17592186152495 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345551 13194139535356 true] [17592186152495 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535356 true] [17592186152495 1100 "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}=-6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." 13194139535356 true] [17592186152495 1100 "$x^{2}y^{″} - 4xy^{′} =-6x^{2}$\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ax^{5} + B$, where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.\nThe function $y_{p}(x) = x$\n{{field-1}}\na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nThe function $y_{p}(x) = x^{2}$\n{{field-2}}\na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\nwhere\n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." 13194139535356 false] [17592186152495 1145 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-4x(1)=-6{x}^{2}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(2x)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(2)-4x(2x)=-6{x}^{2}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345422 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345423 13194139535356 true] [17592186345422 1089 "q-129942" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345422 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345422 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345422 1113 #uuid "2001423a-d37a-5c09-b979-2bab1292dbaf" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345422 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-2({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}-2u{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345424 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345425 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345426 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345427 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345428 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345426 13194139535356 true] [17592186345423 1110 #uuid "97c17e77-38a2-5835-b825-8c5cd3818c0b" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345424 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345424 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345424 1111 #uuid "d005f2e4-bdb4-539e-9319-3dd773e75374" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345425 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345425 1111 #uuid "b5cf3c07-f28f-5d10-81d1-7e1b191c5adc" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345426 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345426 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345426 1111 #uuid "4dd43338-b42d-5aef-9330-1b0efc2d9f45" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345427 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345427 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345427 1111 #uuid "b37b7654-37e8-52f2-85f9-8720ecb27b95" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345428 1034 "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345428 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345428 1111 #uuid "e829a974-05bc-5521-b96a-620251681971" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345430 13194139535356 true] [17592186345429 1089 "q-129956" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345429 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345429 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345429 1113 #uuid "a5120476-e026-5368-b3db-664e0d6dd1eb" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345429 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{-2t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t})+5({u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t})+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}+5{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-10u{e}^{-2t}+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}+{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}({u}^{″}+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\,⟹\\,u=-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{-2t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}={e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{e}^{-5t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345431 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345432 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345433 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345434 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345435 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345435 13194139535356 true] [17592186345430 1110 #uuid "8b9fdc43-d5e0-52de-b2b7-a3656f2ed21f" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345431 1034 "y=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345431 1111 #uuid "5e58b3dd-a3b7-5da9-b4d6-a02626dc371a" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345432 1034 "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-5t}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345432 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345432 1111 #uuid "d3836d7a-f6ee-5631-8faa-2573ee12135f" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345433 1034 "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{t}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345433 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345433 1111 #uuid "5880b1f2-cf03-5a6e-bc6c-2f580523a189" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345434 1034 "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{2t}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345434 1111 #uuid "397ece8b-d486-5771-a65c-269cde3e638d" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345435 1034 "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345435 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345435 1111 #uuid "78ea7268-48ad-5be8-a7f8-8832dcae05fe" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345436 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345437 13194139535356 true] [17592186345436 1089 "q-130003" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345436 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345436 1100 "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345436 1113 #uuid "c1d410c0-047c-5f49-a378-14e326ad53fe" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345436 1145 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x})-x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x)(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}-(x)^{′}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-(1)x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345438 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345439 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345440 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345441 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345442 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345438 13194139535356 true] [17592186345437 1110 #uuid "12818fef-4f6e-5d11-af5d-66cde3336978" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345438 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345438 1111 #uuid "21ca1c98-39d6-58fc-a052-7fd4f4baf3ad" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345439 1034 "I only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345439 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345439 1111 #uuid "044f3745-e5e5-5f33-9c7f-8a79f58b5df9" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345440 1034 "None" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345440 1111 #uuid "b2c67792-2047-5297-a989-42e219275894" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345441 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345441 1111 #uuid "1eb3d7e8-7156-5b95-a41e-ed711ca7d69a" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345442 1034 "II only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345442 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345442 1111 #uuid "d0ee4f66-6b9c-5f9a-b343-aa1a082677b4" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345443 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345444 13194139535356 true] [17592186345443 1089 "q-130005" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345443 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345443 1100 "Consider the differential equation $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+4{x}^{3}y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is a solution to the equation\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345443 1113 #uuid "5007768b-5de4-5206-bbd7-b32aa44bd388" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345443 1145 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=(4{x}^{3}-4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2})-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}=(2x-2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}+(4{x}^{3}-4{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution while ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345445 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345446 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345447 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345448 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345449 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345449 13194139535356 true] [17592186345444 1110 #uuid "fae05613-a3fa-5761-81f5-ba92b39aebb7" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345445 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345445 1111 #uuid "87502948-1837-5a40-a865-25c54b543f6e" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345446 1034 "I only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345446 1111 #uuid "97bf1bb4-c458-5c49-ba18-5f6c6d011526" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345447 1034 "None" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345447 1111 #uuid "e1c4d0ae-8c3e-5e0f-940a-c70f97c87ed1" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345448 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345448 1111 #uuid "a6c0cd14-7d4a-5f7c-bd4a-f8a42411ebd3" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345449 1034 "II only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345449 1111 #uuid "4c2a1047-eaa3-52d0-b208-e2fe3487929b" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345450 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345451 13194139535356 true] [17592186345450 1089 "q-130012" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345450 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535356 true] [17592186345450 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$ is a solution to the equation $2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345450 1113 #uuid "78c4435d-d549-59c7-b588-14428a93b097" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345450 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{-1}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3})+3t({u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2})-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-4{u}^{′}+4u{t}^{-1}+3{u}^{′}-3u{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2t{w}^{′}-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{-1}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}){t}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3/2}\\cdot {t}^{-1}={t}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345452 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345455 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345456 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345456 13194139535356 true] [17592186345451 1110 #uuid "81ad463b-45d7-5172-b18f-aa25b2851e3e" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345452 1034 "y=\\frac{A}{t}+Bt" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345452 1111 #uuid "22e30927-b475-55e2-8df9-d99b889b8e4b" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345453 1034 "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B{t}^{3}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345453 1111 #uuid "f5b2eaf2-55a0-5e5d-b855-0ab1b9b71566" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345454 1034 "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345454 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345454 1111 #uuid "bbfbb4c9-66b3-59c6-a96f-d00ed4335d95" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345455 1034 "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt[3]{t}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345455 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345455 1111 #uuid "c3bdca4b-33bb-55e4-b8b0-0d31cc358add" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345456 1034 "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345456 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345456 1111 #uuid "5def96eb-61e7-5671-990a-4e0cfb7a11f7" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345457 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345458 13194139535356 true] [17592186345457 1089 "q-130017" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345457 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535356 true] [17592186345457 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-4t{y}^{′}+6y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345457 1113 #uuid "b07ce313-36a3-5fa3-94a0-d88524a33c9c" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345457 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-4t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-8u{t}^{2}+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{2}={t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345459 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345460 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345461 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345462 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345458 1110 #uuid "5df7d2ed-a137-509a-be4b-c254ca46333c" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345459 1034 "y=A{t}^{2}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{3}}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345459 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345459 1111 #uuid "0ef1e0dc-62ff-5bd0-990a-8fb1b5cc6f4e" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345460 1034 "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345460 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345460 1111 #uuid "804f03ca-e55e-5f8d-9f7c-23952eb833e9" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345461 1034 "y=A{t}^{2}+\\frac{B}{\\sqrt{t}}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345461 1111 #uuid "a668455f-10cc-5fad-ba36-537a4f50d8bc" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345462 1034 "y=A{t}^{2}+B\\sqrt[3]{t}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345462 1111 #uuid "a4186bac-e04c-5d30-b055-ba5b65a8aa79" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345463 1034 "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345463 1111 #uuid "4f3efed7-029a-5b24-b608-9a9dc4398a2c" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345464 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345465 13194139535356 true] [17592186345464 1089 "q-130040" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345464 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535356 true] [17592186345464 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-15y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345464 1113 #uuid "731e581f-8619-54fb-8401-87d512e8b348" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345464 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)+x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+12{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+12u{x}^{3}+{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+3u{x}^{3}-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+13{u}^{′}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}(2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2{w}^{′}x+13w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-11/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{11}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345466 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345467 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345468 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345469 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345470 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345467 13194139535356 true] [17592186345465 1110 #uuid "aed909bc-e003-59a0-a394-761de917a81c" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345466 1034 "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{x}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345466 1111 #uuid "87644de6-219d-5466-bf46-84f24ad6aed9" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345467 1034 "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345467 1111 #uuid "4c1a4662-08a9-5568-aa2e-6073e37cbd1f" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345468 1034 "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345468 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345468 1111 #uuid "ae1fc8ce-0359-5500-a620-0ced8dea5a7c" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345469 1034 "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{7/2}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345469 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345469 1111 #uuid "e0cc3241-1cbb-54dd-a41a-7daed0977005" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345470 1034 "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{4/3}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345470 1111 #uuid "bfb1932c-268e-5d36-af1b-0a92cc6f22e2" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345472 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345475 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345478 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345483 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345488 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1089 "q-339876" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1100 "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=3{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1113 #uuid "f225fe36-b845-56ed-a087-0e870bbc744e" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345471 1145 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-x})^{′}=-{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n({e}^{-x})+5(-{e}^{-x})+4({e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x})+5({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})+4(x{e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}+x{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345473 13194139535356 true] [17592186345472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345474 13194139535356 true] [17592186345472 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535356 true] [17592186345472 1041 "field-1" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345472 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345473 13194139535356 true] [17592186345472 1110 #uuid "f4ef30e6-dac9-5cb3-a138-7d3370bd4dd2" 13194139535356 true] [1759