$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-6x(1)+10(x)=4x.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(6x)-6x(3{x}^{2})+10({x}^{3})=4x.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345533 13194139535356 true] [17592186345532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345534 13194139535356 true] [17592186345532 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535356 true] [17592186345532 1041 "field-1" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345532 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345534 13194139535356 true] [17592186345532 1110 #uuid "b543d84b-439c-589e-806a-97621bf1d072" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345533 1034 "isn't" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345533 1111 #uuid "9afe2a22-2ebd-5e35-a966-8789acae5f54" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345534 1034 "is" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 1319413953