ce ${t}^{5}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{3}={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{6}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345563 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345564 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345565 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345566 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345567 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345566 13194139535356 true] [17592186345562 1110 #uuid "50a645b1-2d93-5f7f-89d9-479c20e47dea" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345563 1034 "y=A{t}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{3}}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345563 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345563 1111 #uuid "22fd5242-997f-5163-a30e-42db18763971" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345564 1034 "y=A{t}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345564 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345564 1111 #uuid "784414e6-6ca6-5b80-86cf-0d9dbffc192f" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345565 1034 "y=A{t}^{3}+Bt" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345565 1111 #uuid "c163f5a8-7e6c-540a-bca8-09d0bc41a62a" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345566 1034 "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345566 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345566 1111 #uuid "b39c4523-4585-5400-99cc-d04fb7083755" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345567 1034 "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535356 true] [17592186345567 1111 #uuid "a7d55a68-cdf4-5607-9d99-2e68cfd74d07" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345568 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345569 13194139535356 true] [17592186345568 1089 "q-77674" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345568 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345568 1100 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{sin}^{2}⁡x.$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345568 1113 #uuid "58ccb5cc-18e4-5262-8dcb-4e18578a688d" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345568 1145 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x+4({sin}^{2}⁡x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x=2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{sin}^{2}⁡x$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345570 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345571 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345572 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345573 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345574 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345572 13194139535356 true] [17592186345569 1110 #uuid "7ecebc68-c174-5102-b97e-f4cacc16f40b" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345570 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345570 1111 #uuid "5cf0114e-c3da-5f77-bf4e-2049f662a018" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345571 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345571 1111 #uuid "e605c6ca-e514-5b09-bd16-8ef34cda4fc5" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345572 1034 "I only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345572 1111 #uuid "541ff7a0-f988-5200-bbfa-95df094039e4" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345573 1034 "III only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345573 1111 #uuid "20749f07-b461-504a-b2b1-4a44080313f6" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345574 1034 "II only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345574 1111 #uuid "60c8e750-71f9-5373-910d-ef84fff5454d" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345575 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345576 13194139535356 true] [17592186345575 1089 "q-79930" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345575 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535356 true] [17592186345575 1100 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-t}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}.$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345575 1113 #uuid "a4147874-53d3-5715-b4ec-da87549bacb7" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345575 1145 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(t)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t}+2(-{e}^{-t})+{e}^{-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{1-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{1-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-t}+2(-{e}^{1-t})+{e}^{1-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{1-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{-t}{({e}^{1-t})}^{′}-{({e}^{-t})}^{′}{e}^{1-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(-{e}^{1-t})-(-{e}^{-t}){e}^{1-t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2t}+{e}^{1-2t} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0,$ the functions are not linearly independent, hence $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345577 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345578 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345579 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345580 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345581 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345579 13194139535356 true] [17592186345576 1110 #uuid "edbdca44-d986-5693-a9da-2d1de48e3591" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345577 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345577 1111 #uuid "79be48c9-db1b-54e4-9c06-dd1d4380cc56" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345578 1034 "I only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345578 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345578 1111 #uuid "f9f83a0b-62ed-56ef-9a77-c112be4e20e0" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345579 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345579 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345579 1111 #uuid "e014b137-f87e-5727-a4f4-c575320f992e" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345580 1034 "I, II and III" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345580 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345580 11