0a25f22-3baf-52b8-9a99-46b0db20c803" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345596 1145 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345598 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345599 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345600 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345601 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345602 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1041 "selection" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1104 #edb/ref 17592186345601 13194139535356 true] [17592186345597 1110 #uuid "3c3f3166-876e-5faa-b246-3af0a54b9237" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345598 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345598 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345598 1111 #uuid "54b0d0a3-21a1-5df1-861b-513da03dbaea" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345599 1034 "I only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345599 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345599 1111 #uuid "3defc48d-d7e1-5b0e-b671-1730d06d90b8" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345600 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345600 1111 #uuid "a61af580-9f8a-5aaf-ad73-ff407a9c1323" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345601 1034 "I, II and III" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345601 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345601 1111 #uuid "1873399a-0706-582f-933c-ee6a5de25d89" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345602 1034 "II only" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535356 true] [17592186345602 1111 #uuid "888f0ec7-4c54-5724-9db3-d0bed4c700bf" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345603 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345604 13194139535356 true] [17592186345603 1089 "q-90911" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345603 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535356 true] [17592186345603 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535356 true] [17592186345603 1113 #uuid "6142e9d9-60af-53a0-80f6-f2464029d408" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345603 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139535356 true] [17592186345604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345605 13194139535356 t